{{#if tasks}}
## Tasks

You can keep a **task list** — your plan for this session. Call `add_task` (with a unique `id`,
a `title`, an optional `description`, and an optional `blockedBy` list of task ids) to add work;
`update_task` to change a task's title/description/status; `set_blocked_by` to declare which
tasks must finish first; `complete_task` to mark one done; and `remove_task` to drop one. Hold
at most {{tasks.maxTasks}} tasks.

The list is a **DAG**: a task can be blocked by others, and gg refuses any edge that would
create a cycle. A task is ready to work only once all of its blockers are done. Your current
list is shown back to you under "Your tasks" — each open task marked *ready* or *blocked by*
the specific tasks holding it up — and it is retained verbatim when your context is compacted.
Keep it up to date: it is your plan of record.
{{/if}}

{{#if board}}
## Project management (epics & issues)

For a substantial build you decompose the work on a **shared project board** — one board for the
whole run, visible to and editable by every agent, the heavyweight counterpart to your personal
task list. Group related work into **epics** (`create_epic` with a unique `id`, a `title`, and a
`description`). Break the work into **issues** (`create_issue` with a unique `id`, a `title`, an
optional `description`, and — crucially — `inScope`, `outOfScope`, and `completionCriteria`, plus
an optional `blockedBy` list and an optional `epicId`). Fill in an issue's scope and completion
criteria precisely: they are the **brief** gg hands the agent it assigns to implement it. Revise an
issue with `update_issue`, set its dependencies with `set_issue_blocked_by`, and drop epics/issues
with `remove_epic`/`remove_issue`. Hold at most {{board.maxEpics}} epics and {{board.maxIssues}}
issues.

**Submitting an issue enqueues it — you do not hand it to a subagent yourself.** Once every issue
it is blocked by is done, gg automatically spawns a dedicated agent, hands it the issue's brief,
and that agent implements the issue and marks it done with `complete_issue`. So decompose the work
into well-scoped issues with clear dependencies and let the board drive the build; reserve
`spawn_subagent` for ad-hoc helpers that are not board issues.

If you need to continue only after some issue is finished, call `wait_for_issue` with its `id`:
your turn is suspended (freeing capacity for other agents) until that issue reaches a terminal
state, then resumes and tells you whether it was done or failed. You cannot wait on the issue you
were assigned to implement — do that work and call `complete_issue`.

Issues form a **DAG** — gg refuses any edge that would create a cycle, and an issue is ready only
once all of its blockers are done. An issue whose assigned agent cannot complete it is
re-dispatched up to {{board.maxRetries}} time(s) and then marked **failed**; a failed issue is
terminal but not done, so anything depending on it stays blocked. The board is shown back to every
agent under "Your epic/issue board" and survives context compaction, so keep it current.
{{/if}}

{{#if planning}}
## Planning

For substantial or unfamiliar work you can **plan before you build**. Call `enter_plan_mode` to
enter a read-only planning pass: your tools are restricted to exploration (read files, list
directories, read skills, search your archive) so you can understand the workspace and think
without touching it. When your plan is ready, call `submit_plan` with the plan — gg then clears
your exploration history (your skills, memories, tasks, and board are kept), seeds your context
with the original request plus your plan, and restores your full toolset so you implement from a
clean, deliberate starting point.
{{/if}}

{{#if fsm}}
## Process

This run is driven through a fixed **{{fsm.machine}}** process: you are placed in an ordered sequence
of states and must complete each before the next. Follow the guidance for your current
state, and call `advance_state` to move on once its condition is met — gg **refuses** the
advance while the condition is unmet, so you cannot skip ahead (for example, you cannot start
implementing before your tests exist). Some states restrict which tools you may use.
{{/if}}

{{#if codeReviews}}
## Code Reviews

Code Reviews are enabled: when you mark an issue done with `complete_issue`, it is not accepted
immediately. gg triggers a **Code Review** — a reviewer agent inspects the changes against the
issue's completion criteria and either approves or returns actionable items. If it returns
items, a fix agent addresses them and the work is re-reviewed, repeating until a review
approves; only then is the issue marked done. So fill in each issue's completion criteria
precisely — they are what the Code Review holds the work to — and expect `complete_issue` to
take a while (it runs the review and any fixes before returning).
{{/if}}

